vkd3d-proton: the fence worker does not ask the host again about a submission it has seen complete

anyos#1186.  Every ID3D12CommandQueue::Signal enqueues a (submission
timeline, value) entry for the queue's fence worker, and the worker calls
vkWaitSemaphores for each entry in turn.  On the gfxstream guest that call is
a blocking host round trip whose reply the transport polls for with a timer
backoff (0.5 ms quantum): 0.5 to 1 ms each in WoW Forever (kernel context
switch trace: the worker sleeps on a timer for almost every wait), 12 per
frame on average (ICD counters), although a frame has only 4 or 5 submissions
on the graphics queue.  After the last command lists of a frame WoW signals
five fences and the driver its drain fence on the same submission, so the
driver's present waited for six or seven round trips after the GPU had
finished (anyosPresent "pending drain wait" 2.45 ms per frame).

A timeline semaphore never goes back: once a wait has returned for value v on
the queue's submission timeline (cpu_observed_timeline_value, which the worker
already records), a wait for v or a lower value returns at once, so the worker
now completes such an entry without the call.  Order, callbacks and the
values the fences reach are unchanged; device loss is still seen by the next
real wait.  Not used with the NVIDIA fence proxy path.

WoW Forever, Windows 11 ARM64 guest 3840x2160, level 10: the driver's
"pending drain wait" drops from 2.4-2.5 to 1.5-1.8 ms per frame (2.1 in a
heavier session); the in-game rate moves by 0 to +3 FPS, inside the 2-3 FPS
noise between sessions.  tests/d3d12-fence-order.cpp passes with and without
the patch and fails on a build that never waits.

--- a/libs/vkd3d/command.c
+++ b/libs/vkd3d/command.c
@@ -585,7 +585,22 @@
 {
     pthread_mutex_lock(&queue->fence_mutex);
     queue->cpu_observed_timeline_value = max(queue->cpu_observed_timeline_value, timeline);
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&queue->fence_mutex);
+}
+
+/* AnyOS: whether a wait already saw the queue's submission timeline reach
+ * `timeline`.  A timeline semaphore never goes back, so a wait for that value
+ * or a lower one would return at once; on the gfxstream guest it would still
+ * cost a blocking host round trip (0.5 to 1 ms each with the transport's
+ * timer backoff), one per ID3D12CommandQueue::Signal, and the fence worker
+ * runs them one after another. */
+static bool vkd3d_queue_has_observed_cpu_timeline(struct vkd3d_queue *queue, uint64_t timeline)
+{
+    bool ret;
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&queue->fence_mutex);
+    ret = queue->cpu_observed_timeline_value >= timeline;
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&queue->fence_mutex);
+    return ret;
 }
 
 void vkd3d_add_wait_to_all_queues(struct d3d12_device *device, VkSemaphore vk_semaphore, uint64_t value)
@@ -839,7 +854,16 @@
         if (VKD3D_CONFIG_FLAG_IS_SET(BREADCRUMBS) || VKD3D_CONFIG_FLAG_IS_SET(FAULT))
             timeout = 5000000000ull;
 
-        if (d3d12_device_has_slow_cpu_timeline_semaphores(device) &&
+        if (!d3d12_device_has_slow_cpu_timeline_semaphores(device) &&
+            fence->fence_info.vk_semaphore == queue->vkd3d_queue->submission_timeline &&
+            vkd3d_queue_has_observed_cpu_timeline(queue->vkd3d_queue, fence->fence_info.vk_semaphore_value))
+        {
+            /* AnyOS: several signals (the app's fences, the driver's drain
+             * fence) usually name one submission; only the first needs the
+             * host.  See vkd3d_queue_has_observed_cpu_timeline(). */
+            vr = VK_SUCCESS;
+        }
+        else if (d3d12_device_has_slow_cpu_timeline_semaphores(device) &&
             fence->fence_info.vk_semaphore == queue->vkd3d_queue->submission_timeline)
         {
             vr = vkd3d_queue_wait_submission_timeline(queue->vkd3d_queue, fence->fence_info.vk_semaphore_value, timeout);
